  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nWest to northwest flow aloft will lead to an active weather pattern \nthrough at least mid week and possibly lingering into the later part\nof the week. \n\nSouthern edge of storms moving across northern Ohio will graze the\nnorthern counties in the forecast area. Some strong to damaging winds\nwill be possible. This activity should move east of the area fairly\nquickly this evening.\n\nA strengthening low level jet later tonight will likely lead to \nsome additional redevelopment off to our northwest. Several of the \nhi- res models are suggesting this activity will then drop east- \nsoutheast into our area late tonight into early Monday morning. With \nthis being during a less favorable time of day, would generally \nexpect a weakening trend with any storms that do make it into our \narea, but suppose it will be tough to rule out a low end \nsevere/damaging wind threat, especially across northwest portions of \nour area. \n\nRemnants from the potential morning activity could initially affect \ntemperatures. Have made some minor downward adjustments to \ntemperatures as a result, but we will still be quite humid with \nafternoon heat indices in the 95 to 100 degree range. This would \nstill allow for a fair amount of destabilization through the day so \nadditional showers and thunderstorms will be possible later Monday \nafternoon and into the overnight hours as a weak frontal boundary \npushes south toward our area. With strengthening low/mid level flow,\nsome severe storms will again be possible. Damaging wind will be the\nprimary threat, although increasing low level shear could also lead \nto a lower end tornado threat, especially for areas along and north \nof the I-70 corridor. \n\nIn the persistent northwest flow aloft, additional rounds of showers\nand storms will be possible Tuesday and Wednesday as the frontal \nboundary continues to sag slowly south into our area. A chance for \nprecipitation will linger into Thursday and possibly Friday, with \nthe best chances across our southern areas, closer to the remnant \nboundary.\n\nWith high summertime PWs in place and repeated rounds of storms \npossibly tracking over the same locations, flooding concerns will \nincrease as we progress into mid week.",
